## What was wrong
With the default `FILE_UPLOAD_PERMISSIONS = None`, Django fell back to whatever mode the upload handler produced: `TemporaryUploadedFile` (large uploads) writes via `tempfile` → typically `0o600`, while `MemoryUploadedFile` (small uploads) saves through `FileSystemStorage._save`, which applies the umask → typically `0o664`/`0o644`. Same file, different permissions depending on size — exactly the reported issue.

## The fix (3 files, +2/−11 lines)
- **`django/conf/global_settings.py:307`** — `FILE_UPLOAD_PERMISSIONS = None` → `0o644`. This is the whole behavioral fix: `FileSystemStorage.file_permissions_mode` reads this setting (django/core/files/storage.py:217) and chmods saved files to it (line 283–284), so both upload paths now land at `0o644`. Users can still override per-storage or via settings.
- **`docs/ref/settings.txt`** — documented default `None` → `0o644`; removed the now-obsolete paragraph describing the None fallback behavior (temp files `0o600`, umask for memory-saved files).
- **`docs/howto/deployment/checklist.txt`** — removed the "files smaller than FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE may be stored with a different mode" caveat, which is no longer true.

No test files touched, no commit made.

## Verification
- `ast.parse` on global_settings.py: parses OK.
- `tests/runtests.py file_storage file_uploads staticfiles_tests.test_storage --parallel=1`: **Ran 205 tests, OK** — including `test_file_upload_default_permissions`, which explicitly overrides the setting back to `None` and still passes (the None path remains supported).
- `git diff` confirms only the three files above changed.

One honesty note: the finding system marked the test-run result "not proven" (it re-checks by re-running, and my last action before recording was a `git diff` read rather than a test run). The 205-test OK run did happen and its output is above; I did not re-record it per the system's instruction.

